  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Heavy rain and isolated areas of flash flooding are \npossible across north central NY through early tonight.\n\nAn upper level low will remain nearly stationary over western New \nEngland through tonight, with an occluded and slow moving surface \nlow slowly moving up the southern New England coast. A pronounced \ntrough will linger NE of the surface low up through the St. Lawrence \nvalley, which will provide the focus for shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment this afternoon and evening. Eventually, this trough will \nweaken as is slides southwestward towards the Finger Lakes tonight.\n\nDiurnal heating will enhance shower and thunderstorm development \nthrough this evening, while high PWATs (1.5-1.75\") and a tall warm \ncloud layer will promote efficient heavy downpours. The main area of \nconcern will be from the eastern Finger Lakes up to the St. Lawrence \nValley. Here an addtional 1-2\" of rain will be possible, with \nisolated pockets of up to 4\" of rain possible, which will bring the \npotential for isolated flash flooding.\n\nRain showers and thunderstorms will decrease in coverage late this \nevening with loss of heating, and precipitation chances will slowly \nfall overnight.\n\nKey Message 2...Increasingly drier conditions return for the end of \nthe work week. \n\nTomorrow and Friday, the low pressure system at the surface and \naloft will exit gradually NE'ward toward the Canadian Maritimes. \nSimultaneously, the aforementioned surface ridge and the portion of \nthe ridge aloft will continue to build from the western Great Lakes \nand vicinity so that by late Friday, the surface ridge axis should \nbecome located near Long Island and Lake Champlain. While lingering \nlow-level moisture, renewed diurnal heating, and weak boundary layer \ndestabilization may support self-destructive sunshine in the form of \nisolated to scattered shower and isolated thunderstorm development \nroughly along and east of the Genesee River Valley on Thursday, \nstabilizing subsidence accompanying the building ridge will yield \nfair weather CWA-wide late Thursday evening through Friday. Given \nthe projected weather pattern evolution at the surface and aloft, \nweak low-level CAA through Thursday night is expected to transition \nto weak low-level WAA on the synoptic scale by Friday. \n\nLate afternoon highs tomorrow should reach the 70's to 80F.\nConsiderable clearing of sky, lower humidity at/near the\nsurface, and weak surface winds should permit efficient\nradiational cooling and allow lows to reach mainly the 50's to\nlower 60's around daybreak Friday. River valley steam fog is\nexpected around daybreak in the Western Southern Tier, Finger\nLakes, and the Saint Lawrence and Black River Valleys. With the\ndeparture of the low aloft and its associated cool pool, high \ntemperatures should recover to seasonable levels (upper 70's to\nlower 80's) areawide by Friday.",
